Design and why
The harness is a small framework this Idea builds. It fetches Jira data, runs rules such as 'this field must be filled in', and publishes the results. Each report is a check on it. Most checks need only a simple settings file that says which rule to apply to which tickets; custom code is added only for a rule the settings file cannot express. Each report publishes its detail as a Confluence Cloud page and its figures as metric records. A Jira ticket is created only when a person needs to act, so a clean weekly run creates no new ticket.
A new period ticket starts the run. The harness polls Jira on a short interval for new tickets of a configured kind, works out from configuration which report each is for and which period it covers (the weekly check covers the week just ended, the monthly defect report the prior month), and runs it. The ticket is the log: each step is a comment on it. Because the trigger is the ticket, the schedule is changed in the Jira automation rule that creates it, and an extra run is started by creating a ticket. Polling is the assumption because it needs no inbound connection to the host; Phase 0 validates it, and a webhook is the fallback only if polling does not work.
A ticket is run once. Before starting, the harness looks for its own run comment on the ticket and skips a ticket that already has one. No state is kept outside Jira.
Notification is separate from the check. The check outputs findings per team; a notifier maps team to lead from the team map and sends one message per lead. Unknown lead goes to a person.
The monthly defect report is run at the beginning of each month for the prior month. Its distribution list stays configuration, not code.
Spot-check by exception: each monthly defect report is compared with the prior month; a change beyond a documented bound, or a missing input, holds publication for a person. Everything else publishes.
Workflow-validator diagnosis stays human. The report names the team, filter and count; it does not claim a cause.
The weekly risk report lists owners on an unrestricted page (Eric). It lists risks in the PS, PM and PSC projects that were created more than two weeks ago and whose status is not Accept, Mitigate or Closed, and risks whose status is not Closed that have not been updated in 90 days. The owner is the assignee, or the reporter when the assignee is blank. The page lists key, project, owner, status, dates and the check failed, and never a risk's title or description. The run comment on the ticket has counts only, and the AI summary uses counts and never owner names. It shares its rule definitions with the Risk & Opportunities Monthly Audit.
Summary of the findings, approved and trained. Each report gets a short AI-drafted summary of trends and notable points. The script computes the comparisons (against the previous period, and for the monthly report against the same month last year, the largest movements, and anything outside the bound); the model only writes them up in plain words, from those computed facts and nothing else. Every number in the draft is checked against the facts. The report's figures publish when the run finishes; the draft is posted on that period's ticket, and an Odd Squad team member approves it by running a Jira transition before the text is added to the page, labelled as AI-drafted. A decline transition, with a reason, publishes nothing that period. Only the group allowed to run the transition can approve, and Jira records who did. The summary states no cause and uses team-level figures only, so for the risk report it uses counts and never owner names. Each report has a Markdown guidance file in the scripts' repository, which Odd Squad team members edit to train the next run; the fixed rules in the code cannot be loosened by it, and a preview command shows the draft for the latest period without posting it. Earlier periods are read back from the stored metric records.
Each run is recorded as comments on its period ticket, linking the Confluence page and recording whether a summary was drafted, approved, declined or dropped, with the model, prompt version, guidance file version and approver.
The common display. Every figure a report produces is written as a metric record in the format designed in Phase 0, and shown in the viewer built in Phase 8 through the team map from Phase 7. Phase 0 also compares the platforms for the viewer (RideShare, a generated static site, a Confluence page tree, Datadog links) and analyses whether DORA is linked to in Datadog or recomputed. A count rolls up by adding, a rate by dividing the sums, and a median from samples. A past period rolls up through the team structure of its own period.
Decisions
A run starts when Jira creates that period's ticket (Eric), so the schedule is a Jira automation rule and the ticket holds the log. A managed host that polls for new tickets, and the shared hosting decision, are still required.
Polling Jira is the assumed way to notice new tickets (Eric), to be validated in Phase 0.
Notifications are sent automatically once the template and the team map are approved by Eric. This is deterministic automation, not AI, so there is no per-run approval; a change to either is approved before it is used.
Reports are published to Confluence Cloud, in the general instance (Eric), as unrestricted pages. There is no Word export. Pages are not restricted, including the weekly risk report (Eric).
Because the Confluence pages are readable by everyone, they carry team-level figures and issue keys and no personal names. The figures are not private data (Eric). The one exception is the weekly risk report, which lists owners (Eric).
Each report carries an AI-drafted summary of trends and notable points (Eric). It is generated from figures the script computed, checked, labelled, and never states a cause.
A person approves each summary before it is published, by a Jira transition on the period's ticket (Eric). The report's figures are not held up by the approval.
The summary is trainable (Eric): Odd Squad team members edit a Markdown guidance file to change what is emphasised, always shown or avoided, and the next run follows it. The approval of each summary is the control on the file, so a change to it needs no separate review.
All logging is comments on the period tickets, including that AI was used and with which model, prompt version and guidance file version, and who approved the summary; no log files are written.
The report names, the filters, the ticket-to-report mapping and the distribution are configuration, because the migration of the Jira projects to Atlassian Cloud will change URLs, field IDs and filter names; the approval transition names are configuration too.
Stephanie is the expert for the weekly required-fields, bi-weekly structure and weekly risk adherence reports, and Nicolette for the monthly defect adherence report.
This is the first Idea worked on and there is no existing harness, so the harness is designed in Phase 0 and built in Phase 1 of this Idea.
The weekly risk adherence report is Phase 6 (AUTO-323) (Eric). It takes about an hour a week today (Eric).
A risk is 'not assessed' when it is more than two weeks old and its status is not Accept, Mitigate or Closed, and its owner is the assignee, or the reporter when the assignee is blank (Eric).
There is one common way to show metrics (Eric), designed here and used by AUTO-188, AUTO-106, AUTO-103, AUTO-84 to AUTO-87, AUTO-290 and AUTO-27. It is part of this Idea because this is the first Idea worked on.
The team map comes from the TDIR project (Eric).
The viewer shows the names of managers, directors and segment heads (Eric), because names are the easiest. It never shows an individual engineer.
Everyone can see all levels (Eric).
All team sizes are shown, with no minimum (Eric).
The viewer platform is chosen in Phase 0 by comparison; RideShare is the lead option (Eric).
Whether DORA is linked to in Datadog or recomputed is decided after an analysis in Phase 0 (Eric).
Non-negotiables
A check that cannot be computed (source unreachable, empty source) is reported as unavailable with the missing input named; it is never reported as zero findings.
Every report states its source, the period and when it was obtained.
A Confluence page that is readable by everyone contains no personal names, except the weekly risk report, which lists owners and never copies a risk's title or description. The viewer shows the names of managers, directors and segment heads, and never an individual engineer or team member.
Never publish to the defect distribution when the exception bound trips; hold and notify the owner.
One rule definition shared with PAR and with the Risk & Opportunities audit where the rule is the same; no second, divergent definition.
No notification is sent without an approved template and a confirmed lead; a failed send is recorded and queued, never silently dropped.
An AI summary is never published until a person has approved it by the transition. It states only what the computed figures support and never a cause, is labelled as AI-drafted, receives only team-level computed figures (no names, no ticket text), and its failure or a wait for approval never blocks the report's figures. The guidance file cannot override these rules, and the agent never edits it.
A ticket is never run twice, and a ticket that is not one of the configured kinds never starts a run.
Logging: comments on the period ticket, with no PII, secrets, recipient addresses, owner names or raw payloads. No log files are written to disk. One helper file per external system.
No personal credentials in unattended runs.
A metric record has no field for a person, and no view or code path shows a value for an individual.
A rollup is computed from counts, numerators and denominators or samples, never by averaging averages, and a group with a missing team says so; a missing team is never counted as zero.
Teams and groups are never ranked.
A past period rolls up through the structure of its own period.
Acceptance criteria
Given the weekly field check, the count per team equals a manual run of the same saved filter on the same day.
Given a source that fails, the report for that team shows unavailable and names the missing input.
Given a clean week, no new ticket is created and the run is recorded as comments on the period ticket.
Given a team with findings and a known lead, one notification per lead is sent automatically from the approved template; given no known lead, the finding goes to a person.
Given the start of a month, the defect report is run for the prior month; given a month where the defect figures move beyond the documented bound, publication is held and the owner is notified; given a month within bounds, it publishes without a manual step.
No notification, ticket or comment contains a recipient address list or a personal name that is not already in an authorised report.
Given a published Confluence page, it contains no personal names, except the weekly risk report, which lists owners and contains no risk title or description.
Given the first parallel runs, Stephanie confirms the weekly, structure and risk results and Nicolette confirms the monthly defect figures against their own, or each difference has a recorded reason.
Given a risk created more than two weeks ago whose status is not Accept, Mitigate or Closed, or a risk not Closed that was last updated more than 90 days ago, the weekly risk report lists it with its owner, the assignee or else the reporter.
Given a new period ticket, the matching report runs for the right period without anyone starting it; given a ticket that already has a run comment, it is not run again; given a ticket that is not a configured kind, nothing runs.
Given a change to the schedule in the Jira automation rule, the next run follows the new schedule with no change to the harness.
Given a report with an earlier period, the drafted summary states the comparison and the largest movements, each number in it appears in the computed facts, and it states no cause; given a draft that fails the number check, nothing is posted for approval.
Given a drafted summary, the figures are already published and the page carries no summary until a person runs the approval transition; given the transition, the approved text is added, labelled as AI-drafted, and the person who ran it is recorded on the ticket; given the decline transition, nothing is published.
Given an edit to a report's guidance file asking for something to be shown every time, the next draft includes it if the script computes it, and the run's comment records the file version; given a request the script cannot meet, the run completes and lists it as unmet.
Given a report run, its figures are stored as metric records, each with its team, period, numerator, denominator, source, when obtained and definition version, and no person.
Given a metric, the viewer shows it at the IT Org, segment, director, manager and team levels, and a count at one level equals the sum of the level below.
Given a group where a team has no value, the viewer shows 'n of m teams reporting' and does not count the team as zero.
Given a team map from TDIR, every team has a manager, director and segment or is listed as incomplete, and anything unmapped is shown as Unassigned.
Given a reorganisation in TDIR, a past period shows its own structure.
Given a run, no log file is written.
